This project checks a balance-sheet identity: of the filings a period says should
balance, which do, which cannot be checked, and which do not. The second and third
are different answers and a reader that reports only a total cannot tell them
apart.

NO AFFILIATION WITH THE SECURITIES AND EXCHANGE COMMISSION

This project is not affiliated with, endorsed by, sponsored by or connected to the
United States Securities and Exchange Commission or any other regulator. It is a
consumer of a dataset the Commission publishes, and nothing more.

This section survived a rename and is not vestigial. The distribution was briefly
called `sec-balance-audit`, which parses as *an audit by the SEC* -- and that
reading is why it was renamed, before any release. The name no longer carries the
regulator; the package still reads the Commission's data and still has the word
`audit` in it, so both halves of this notice still have work to do.

"AUDIT" HERE IS NOT A FINANCIAL STATEMENT AUDIT

A financial statement audit is a regulated engagement performed by licensed
auditors under professional standards. This is not one and cannot be one. It reads
published figures and reports whether a single arithmetic identity holds in them.
It does not examine records, it does not form an opinion, and nothing it produces
is an audit opinion, accounting advice or investment advice. A discrepancy it
reports is an arithmetic fact about what was filed; what that fact means is not a
question this software answers.

THIRD-PARTY CONTENT: REAL FIGURES, PSEUDONYMOUS FILERS

The files under `evidence/` are DERIVED from the SEC's Financial Statement Data
Sets, which are United States Government work and carry no copyright. The
derivation is `battery/fetch_sec_quarter.py`; the source archive is not
redistributed here.

Every figure in that evidence is as filed, and the discrepancies reported are real
discrepancies in real published balance sheets. Six of them do not balance. The
FILERS are not named: accession numbers and registrants carry stable pseudonyms,
because publishing a list of identified companies beside an arithmetic error is not
something this package needs to do to make its point. The arithmetic is the
subject, and who filed it changes no test here.

THAT IS PSEUDONYMITY AND NOT ANONYMITY, and it is not offered as any. The source is
public and the scheme is positional, so anyone holding the same quarterly file can
recover the mapping by sorting it, and `--named` emits it directly. What is not
published here is the mapping.

AND THE PSEUDONYMS ARE THE WEAKER HALF OF THAT, WHICH IS WORTH SAYING PLAINLY. The
figures are a join key. 93% of the rows here carry a combination of reported values
unique within the quarter, so a reader holding the same public file can match a row
to a filer by its numbers alone, without touching the labels at all. No labelling
scheme closes that and none is claimed to. It was measured rather than assumed, and
replacing the pseudonyms with an irreversible digest was tried and reverted: it left
this channel exactly as open, cost the byte-for-byte reproducibility the positional
scheme exists for, and the prior labelling stayed in the repository's history where
the figures join the two together anyway.

The figures are reported exactly because a discrepancy of one dollar, rounded, is not
a report of anything. So the honest statement is the narrow one: this repository is
not a searchable list of identified companies beside an arithmetic error, and that is
all the pseudonyms buy.

Nothing in that evidence is an allegation. A balance sheet that does not balance by
one dollar is a balance sheet that does not balance by one dollar, and what that
means is not a question this software answers.

Anyone re-deriving the evidence is making requests to sec.gov and is subject to its
fair-access policy, which requires a declared User-Agent naming a contact. See
`evidence/README.md`.

